17dc38e9-460d-4ac8-b0be-a88137e01f88	Psychology	Dunning-Kruger Effect	Incompetent people overestimate their ability.	A metacognitive failure where low-ability individuals lack the skills needed to recognize their lack of skill. It creates a false sense of superiority.	["Metacognition requires baseline competence.", "Knowledge is a feedback loop.", "The less you know, the simpler the problem seems."]	["Like a broken speedometer that always reads 100mph, regardless of actual speed."]	["First Principles Deconstructor", "Epistemic Humility"]	["Hiring: Beware overconfident candidates.", "Investing: Recognize when you are in a bubble."]	["How does the internet amplify this?", "What is the peak of Mount Stupid?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
6bc2e80a-8af1-47ef-a081-c0f05c482e61	Psychology	Cognitive Dissonance	The mental discomfort of conflicting beliefs.	When beliefs contradict actions, the brain experiences discomfort. To resolve it, we usually change beliefs to justify the action, rather than changing the action.	["The brain seeks consistency.", "Ego protection > truth.", "Rationalization is a defense mechanism."]	["Like a smoker convincing themselves the science is flawed, just to keep smoking in peace."]	["Sunk Cost Fallacy", "Confirmation Bias"]	["Post-purchase rationalization.", "Cult recruitment dynamics."]	["How do marketers exploit this?", "How do you break out of a dissonant loop?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
d2f92dc0-6826-4d0d-9118-46d34978af74	Biology	Homeostasis	The body's tendency to maintain internal stability.	The body is constantly fighting entropy. It uses feedback loops to regulate temperature, blood sugar, and pH.	["Systems require stability.", "Feedback loops push toward equilibrium.", "Everything is connected."]	["Like a smart thermostat turning the AC on and off to keep the room at 72 degrees."]	["The Feedback Loop", "Supply and Demand"]	["Health: Why sleep and diet matter.", "Business: Why markets seek equilibrium."]	["What happens when homeostasis fails?", "How do biological systems scale?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
2babfd83-19f1-4cf6-bf58-bcb94ee2b39f	Biology	The Mitochondrial Engine	How cells generate energy.	Mitochondria act as powerhouses. They take in nutrients, break them down, and create energy-rich molecules (ATP).	["Energy is transferred, not created.", "Cellular processes require energy.", "Efficiency dictates survival."]	["Think of mitochondria as tiny coal power plants inside a factory converting food into electricity."]	["Energy Constraints", "Systems Thinking"]	["Health: Improving mitochondrial function.", "Aging: Mitochondrial decay."]	["How do mitochondria communicate?", "What is their evolutionary origin?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
81de855e-dc6d-4f8f-bb52-5a3a2eb04dde	Physics	Entropy	The universe tends toward disorder.	The Second Law of Thermodynamics states that in a closed system, disorder (entropy) always increases. You need energy to maintain order.	["Energy is required for order.", "Closed systems tend toward chaos.", "You can only break even."]	["Like a bedroom that gets messy automatically. You must expend energy to clean it."]	["Bottlenecks & Constraints", "Homeostasis"]	["Life: Why we age.", "Business: Why companies become bureaucratic."]	["What is the heat death of the universe?", "How does local entropy decrease?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
7a5bb16b-d3e0-4f63-a270-ec2f88ee8232	Physics	The Butterfly Effect	Tiny changes have massive consequences.	In chaos theory, complex systems are highly sensitive to initial conditions. A tiny error changes the output completely.	["Complex systems are non-linear.", "Long-term prediction is impossible.", "Small actions compound."]	["A butterfly flapping its wings in Brazil causing a tornado in Texas weeks later."]	["Compounding Interest", "Systems Thinking"]	["Weather forecasting limits.", "Life: Why small habits matter."]	["How do we make decisions in chaos?", "Chaos vs randomness?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
7f386078-b34a-4148-9c67-07a3389a65ef	Economics	Opportunity Cost	The cost of the alternative you gave up.	Every choice has a cost. The true cost is not just the money spent, but the value of the best alternative not pursued.	["Resources are scarce.", "Choosing A means rejecting B.", "Every decision has a hidden cost."]	["Choosing to watch 4 hours of Netflix. The cost is the book you could have written."]	["Inversion", "Trade-offs"]	["Time management.", "Capital allocation."]	["How do you calculate it in relationships?", "Why do people ignore sunk costs?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
278276c5-32d5-4b0c-9d1e-9b3b86df4d10	Economics	The Network Effect	The value of a product increases as more people use it.	A product becomes more valuable as users grow. It creates monopolies and powerful moats.	["Metcalfe's Law: Value = n^2.", "More nodes = more connections.", "Self-reinforcing flywheel."]	["A telephone. Useless if you are the only one with one, essential if everyone has one."]	["The Flywheel", "Systems Thinking"]	["Why Facebook is hard to beat.", "How to build a network effect in your career."]	["What is the Cold Start Problem?", "Can network effects become negative?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
4e23875a-1495-4c3d-b138-52e2cb02a1cd	Technology	Moore's Law	Transistors double every two years.	Computing power roughly doubles every two years while cost halves. It drove the exponential growth of the digital age.	["Exponential growth is deceptive.", "Miniaturization requires engineering leaps.", "Digital is built on physical constraints."]	["Like compound interest, but for computing power."]	["Compounding Interest", "Entropy"]	["Predicting tech trends.", "Why AI is exploding now."]	["What happens at physical limits?", "How does quantum fit in?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
0fa1fc64-83b1-48db-8240-ad0ef49c9601	Sociology	The Diffusion of Innovations	How new ideas spread.	Rogers theorized ideas spread in a bell curve: Innovators (2.5%), Early Adopters (13.5%), Early Majority (34%), Late Majority (34%), Laggards (16%).	["Societies resist change.", "Social proof drives adoption.", "The Chasm is deadly."]	["A fashion trend. First influencers, then cool kids, then everyone."]	["The Tipping Point", "Network Effects"]	["Launching new products.", "Spreading culture."]	["How do you cross the chasm?", "Why do great products fail?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
15c9cd09-c95e-442c-b0ee-7616c4ec8127	Sociology	The Paradox of Choice	More options lead to less happiness.	Barry Schwartz found too many options paralyze people and make them less satisfied with their choices.	["Limited processing power.", "More options = higher cognitive load.", "Perfection expectation rises."]	["Standing in a supermarket with 100 types of jam. You buy none due to fear."]	["Cognitive Load", "Bottlenecks"]	["UX Design: Less is more.", "Life: Constraints create freedom."]	["How do you design a menu?", "Maximizer vs Satisficer?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
63b0274a-6f1c-4c7e-b3f2-585a2f7c0e27	Philosophy	Stoicism	Focus only on what you can control.	We cannot control external events, only our judgments. By mastering our mind, we achieve peace regardless of circumstances.	["Dichotomy of Control.", "Emotions are judgments.", "Virtue is the only true good."]	["A ship captain. You cannot control the storm, but you can adjust the sails."]	["The Feedback Loop", "Systems Thinking"]	["Stress management.", "Leadership under pressure."]	["Stoicism vs suppression?", "Role of logic in emotion?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
5409ddee-be85-4921-b115-dcd3da7eb276	History	The Great Man Theory	History is shaped by exceptional individuals.	The theory that history can be largely explained by the impact of great men or heroes; highly influential individuals who used their power to make a decisive historical impact.	["Individuals can alter the trajectory of systems.", "Power concentrates in charismatic nodes.", "Context enables the individual."]	["Like a single boulder in a river. It changes the flow of the water around it, but the water was already flowing."]	["Systems Thinking", "The Network Effect"]	["Leadership studies.", "Analyzing modern tech founders."]	["Does the hero make the time, or does the time make the hero?", "How does this theory ignore systemic factors?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
2d22cd96-6ad2-4005-b2c0-f06255d2f137	History	Path Dependence	History matters. Past choices constrain future ones.	The phenomenon where a system becomes locked into a specific path of development because of decisions made in the past, even if those past decisions are no longer optimal.	["Switching costs increase over time.", "Early decisions compound.", "Optimization is not always the goal; survival is."]	["Like the QWERTY keyboard. It was designed to slow typists down to prevent jams, but we still use it today because learning a new layout is too expensive."]	["Sunk Cost Fallacy", "Entropy"]	["Standardization (USB-C, VHS vs Betamax).", "Understanding corporate legacy systems."]	["How do you break out of a suboptimal path?", "What role do open standards play in path dependence?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
c3c6e6e0-a355-48d2-96c8-b50ef39087c8	Engineering	The Iron Triangle	Fast, Cheap, Good. Pick two.	A project management model that asserts you cannot simultaneously have high quality, low cost, and fast speed. You must sacrifice one to achieve the other two.	["Resources are finite.", "Time is finite.", "Quality requires resources and time."]	["Like building a house. You can build it fast and cheap (but it will be ugly), or fast and high quality (but it will cost a fortune), or high quality and cheap (but it will take years)."]	["Opportunity Cost", "Bottlenecks"]	["Software development (The Mythical Man-Month).", "Contract negotiation."]	["Is quality actually a fixed variable?", "How does AI change the Iron Triangle?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
43916f96-39c9-4fb7-a62c-c2fc15481228	Engineering	The Redundancy Principle	Backup systems prevent catastrophic failure.	In systems architecture, redundancy is the duplication of critical components or functions of a system with the intention of increasing reliability of the system, usually in the form of a backup or fail-safe.	["Single points of failure are dangerous.", "Complexity increases with redundancy.", "Failures are inevitable; outages are optional."]	["Like having a spare tire in your car. You hope you never need it, but if you do, it prevents a small issue from becoming a disaster."]	["Antifragility", "Entropy"]	["Server backups.", "Personal finance (emergency funds)."]	["How much redundancy is too much?", "What is the difference between redundancy and resilience?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
31cc5c76-1faf-4952-a150-8adb58786285	Strategy	OODA Loop	Observe, Orient, Decide, Act.	A decision-making cycle developed by military strategist John Boyd. The goal is to cycle through OODA faster than an opponent, causing them to become confused and reactive.	["Speed of adaptation beats speed of execution.", "Orientation (context) is the most critical step.", "Feedback loops must be fast."]	["Like a dogfight between two fighter jets. The pilot who sees, thinks, and moves faster wins."]	["The Feedback Loop", "The Iron Triangle"]	["Business agility.", "Competitive analysis."]	["How does information overload affect OODA?", "Why is the Orient step the most important?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
57c5420b-87f3-4b45-b15f-7acf771e30a5	Strategy	The Law of Shitty Clickthroughs	All marketing channels degrade over time.	As a marketing channel matures, the effectiveness of the initial tactics degrades. What worked in the beginning (novelty, low competition) stops working as more people adopt the tactic.	["Attention is finite.", "Users build ad-blindness.", "The early bird gets the worm, but the second mouse gets the cheese."]	["Like a new shortcut through a field. At first, it is fast and empty. Eventually, everyone uses it and it becomes a traffic jam."]	["Entropy", "The Diffusion of Innovations"]	["Performance marketing.", "Social media strategy."]	["How do you find the next under-priced channel?", "Is there such a thing as a permanent channel?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
612b8a57-602d-49ec-93a5-3f78686c2472	Human Behavior	The Mere Exposure Effect	Familiarity breeds liking.	People tend to develop a preference for things merely because they are familiar with them. It is a psychological phenomenon by which people tend to develop a preference for things merely because they are familiar with them.	["The brain prefers cognitive ease.", "Familiarity equals safety.", "Repetition builds trust."]	["Like a song you hate at first, but after hearing it on the radio 10 times, you start to like it."]	["Confirmation Bias", "The Paradox of Choice"]	["Brand advertising.", "Dating and relationships."]	["Does familiarity always breed liking?", "How does the mere exposure effect work with negative news?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
e84c5afd-18ea-45c9-bd9e-3c1b2b957041	Human Behavior	Hanlon's Razor	Never attribute to malice what can be explained by stupidity.	A philosophical razor that suggests we should assume ignorance or incompetence before assuming ill intent when explaining the actions of others.	["Intentions are hard to read.", "Complex systems produce errors.", "Assuming malice escalates conflict."]	["Like getting cut off in traffic. Instead of assuming the driver is evil and out to get you, assume they are just distracted or careless."]	["Cognitive Dissonance", "Stoicism"]	["Conflict resolution.", "Product design (user errors vs user malice)."]	["Is it always stupidity?", "What is the difference between stupidity and systemic failure?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
39732fea-3477-4a3c-8451-3ef5c07e6060	Mathematics	The Normal Distribution	The Bell Curve.	A probability distribution that is symmetric about the mean, showing that data near the mean are more frequent in occurrence than data far from the mean.	["Most events cluster around an average.", "Extremes are rare.", "The Central Limit Theorem drives this."]	["Like human height. Most people are average height. Very tall and very short people are rare."]	["Power Law", "The Paradox of Choice"]	["Standardized testing.", "Quality control."]	["Why does the Bell Curve fail in finance?", "What happens when the average is not the goal?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
8cf9b082-abe7-47d9-9613-83e4c09d7cd8	Mathematics	The Power Law	The 80/20 Rule.	A functional relationship between two quantities, where a relative change in one quantity results in a proportional relative change in the other quantity, independent of the initial size of those quantities.	["Winner takes all.", "Extremes dominate the average.", "Scale-free networks."]	["Like wealth distribution. The top 1% hold vastly more wealth than the bottom 50% combined."]	["The Network Effect", "Pareto Principle"]	["Venture capital (investing in unicorns).", "Content creation (viral hits)."]	["Why do power laws emerge in networks?", "How do you plan for a power law distribution?"]	2026-11-05 10:18:48.202863+00
55ffa200-fa02-4fd0-be1e-b788e1a52eb1	Computer Science	The Halting Problem	You cannot predict if a program will finish.	Proven by Alan Turing in 1936, the Halting Problem states that it is impossible to write a program that can determine whether any given program will eventually halt (finish) or run forever, given a specific input. It exposes the fundamental limits of computation.	["Computation has absolute limits.", "Self-reference creates paradoxes.", "Prediction requires simulation, which takes time."]	["Like asking a fortune teller if a coin will land heads or tails. To know for sure, they have to wait for the coin to land, defeating the purpose of prediction."]	["Gödel's Incompleteness Theorems", "The Butterfly Effect"]	["Debugging: Why you cannot build a perfect anti-virus.", "AI Safety: Why we cannot perfectly predict AI behavior."]	["How does the Halting Problem impact compiler optimization?", "What problems are decidable?"]	2026-11-05 10:32:14.597972+00
adbb18e8-91cc-4be4-a6bd-49648b4a709d	Computer Science	The CAP Theorem	You cannot have it all in a distributed system.	The CAP Theorem states that a distributed data store can only guarantee two of three properties: Consistency (every read gets the latest write), Availability (every request gets a response), and Partition Tolerance (the system works despite network failures).	["Networks are inherently unreliable.", "You must make trade-offs.", "Physical distance creates latency."]	["Like a group project. If you want everyone (Availability) to have the exact same document at the same time (Consistency), you cannot work if someone's internet drops (Partition). You must choose."]	["The Iron Triangle", "Entropy"]	["Database Design: Choosing between SQL (Consistency) and NoSQL (Availability).", "System Architecture."]	["Why is Partition Tolerance mandatory?", "How do systems like Google Spanner achieve both?"]	2026-11-05 10:32:14.597972+00
