ef74d538-df69-44ed-aaab-3d4208e666c4	\N	Long vs Short — The Core Rule	1	\N	\r\n# 🎵 Long vs Short — The Core Rule\r\n\r\nDutch vowels come in two sizes: **short** and **long**. This one rule unlocks the entire sound system.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Rule\r\n\r\n> One vowel letter = **short** sound.\r\n>\r\n> Two vowel letters = **long** sound.\r\n\r\n| Dutch | Meaning | Sound |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| man | man | short A |\r\n| maan | moon | long A |\r\n| bom | bomb | short O |\r\n| boom | tree | long O |\r\n| bed | bed | short E |\r\n| been | leg | long E |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 Why It Matters\r\n\r\n**man** and **maan** are different words. **bom** and **boom** are different words. Get the length wrong → you say a different word.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 03 Closed vs Open Syllables\r\n\r\n| Pattern | Vowel | Example |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Vowel + Consonant at end | short | *man* |\r\n| Vowel + Vowel | long | *maan* |\r\n| Vowel at end of word | long | *ga* |\r\n| Vowel + single consonant + vowel | long | *maken* |\r\n\r\nOpen syllable (ends in a vowel sound) → long.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 04 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Two vowels: **stretch**. One vowel: **clip**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 🎉 Side Quest Complete\r\n\r\nTest your understanding before moving on.\r\n  	2026-10-14 09:45:03.905328+00	38203b3c-8f0b-4ece-9f3c-3f7686a768c0	f	80
8180d10b-b380-44cd-ba08-06b7b4aef838	\N	OO — The Famous "OH" Sound	2	\N	\r\n# 🅾️ OO — The Famous "OH" Sound\r\n\r\nThe single most important Dutch vowel for English speakers.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🚨 01 The Trap\r\n\r\nIn English, **OO** sounds like *"food"* or *"book"*. In Dutch, **OO** sounds like *"boat"* — a clean "OH".\r\n\r\n| English | Dutch | Dutch sounds like |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| book | boek | *book* (spelled OE) |\r\n| boot | boot | *boat* |\r\n| tree | boom | *bohm* |\r\n| red | rood | *rohd* |\r\n| big | groot | *groht* |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 The Rule\r\n\r\n> Dutch **OO** = English **"OH"** (as in "go", "boat").\r\n>\r\n> If you want the English "oo" sound, Dutch uses **OE**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 03 Two Families\r\n\r\n**OO family (long "OH")**\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| rood | red |\r\n| brood | bread |\r\n| groot | big |\r\n| boom | tree |\r\n| boot | boat |\r\n| doos | box |\r\n| oog | eye |\r\n| oor | ear |\r\n\r\n**OE family (English "oo")**\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| boek | book |\r\n| zoek | search |\r\n| goed | good |\r\n| moet | must |\r\n| voet | foot |\r\n| groen | green |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 04 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **OO = "OH"** — round your lips then say "oh".\r\n>\r\n> **OE = "OO"** — the sound in English "book".\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 🎉 Side Quest Complete\r\n\r\nTest your understanding before moving on.\r\n  	2026-10-14 09:45:03.905328+00	38203b3c-8f0b-4ece-9f3c-3f7686a768c0	f	80
16c9818f-8a2b-49c6-8396-220506b5a20c	\N	OE — The English "OO"	3	\N	\r\n# 📖 OE — The English "OO"\r\n\r\nNow the other half. **OE** gives you the sound English speakers expect from "OO".\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Rule\r\n\r\n> Dutch **OE** = English **"OO"** (as in "book", "food").\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English | Sounds like |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| boek | book | *book* |\r\n| goed | good | *goot* |\r\n| moet | must | *moot* |\r\n| voet | foot | *foot* |\r\n| groen | green | *groon* |\r\n| zoet | sweet | *soot* |\r\n| bloem | flower | *bloom* |\r\n| koek | cake | *cook* |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔄 02 The Trade\r\n\r\n| English | Dutch |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| book (OO) | boek (OE) |\r\n| boat (OA) | boot (OO) |\r\n| moon (OO) | maan (AA) |\r\n\r\nEvery language made its own choices. Dutch is internally consistent.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧱 03 OE Words to Own\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| boek | book |\r\n| goed | good |\r\n| moet | must |\r\n| voet | foot |\r\n| groen | green |\r\n| zoet | sweet |\r\n| bloem | flower |\r\n| koek | cake |\r\n| snoep | candy |\r\n| hoek | corner |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 04 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **Never write "book" in Dutch.** Always **boek**. OE → "oo".\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 🎉 Side Quest Complete\r\n\r\nTest your understanding before moving on.\r\n  	2026-10-14 09:45:03.905328+00	38203b3c-8f0b-4ece-9f3c-3f7686a768c0	f	80
50220938-4f25-4745-8a4f-318f2011ddf2	\N	Reading Practice — Pronouns in Action	9	\N	\r\n# 📖 Reading Practice — Pronouns in Action\r\n\r\nEverything from this saga in real sentences.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 Short Sentences\r\n\r\n**1.** Ik ben moe.\r\n**2.** Hij heeft een hond.\r\n**3.** Mijn vader werkt hier.\r\n**4.** Zij ziet mij.\r\n**5.** Wij wonen in een klein huis.\r\n**6.** Hun hond is oud.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 Medium Sentences\r\n\r\n**7.** Ik ga morgen met mijn broer naar school.\r\n**8.** Zij drinkt elke ochtend koffie in de keuken.\r\n**9.** Onze ouders wonen in een groot huis.\r\n**10.** Hij leest een boek en ik lees een krant.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Questions\r\n\r\n**11.** Lees jij elke dag?\r\n**12.** Waar woont hij?\r\n**13.** Wat doe jij?\r\n**14.** Hebben jullie een hond?\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Identify the Pronouns\r\n\r\n- *Ik ben moe.* → subject\r\n- *Zij ziet mij.* → subject + object\r\n- *Mijn vader* → possessive\r\n- *Ons huis* → possessive\r\n- *Wij wonen hier.* → subject\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Pronouns tell you who does what to whom.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 🎉 Side Quest Complete\r\n\r\nNext: Saga 1.4 exam.\r\n  	2026-10-14 09:49:06.557687+00	7965201b-549b-42fd-b3db-5495c19d7716	f	80
91cf0b5d-3d87-415f-bfd8-19de88001403	\N	SAGA 1.4 EXAM — Pronoun & Article Mastery	10	\N	\r\n# 🏆 SAGA 1.4 EXAM — Pronoun & Article Mastery\r\n\r\nFull pronoun and article system tested.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📋 Exam Structure\r\n\r\n| Section | Questions | Topic |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| A | 1–4 | Articles |\r\n| B | 5–9 | Pronouns |\r\n| C | 10–13 | Three frames |\r\n| D | 14–17 | Word order |\r\n| E | 18–20 | Questions |\r\n\r\n**Total: 20 questions. Pass: 65% (13/20).**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 🎉 Ready?\r\n\r\nClick **Finish Mission → Take Exam** to begin.\r\n  	2026-10-14 09:49:06.557687+00	7965201b-549b-42fd-b3db-5495c19d7716	t	65
7b7bfb64-e446-41cb-8410-ee0ee35b9e0e	\N	SAGA 1.2 EXAM — Sound Engine Mastery	10	\N	\r\n# 🏆 SAGA 1.2 EXAM — Sound Engine Mastery\r\n\r\nFull vowel system tested.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📋 Exam Structure\r\n\r\n| Section | Questions | Topic |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| A | 1–5 | Long vs short |\r\n| B | 6–10 | OO vs OE |\r\n| C | 11–15 | AA / EE / UU / IE / IJ |\r\n| D | 16–18 | UI / OU / AU |\r\n| E | 19–20 | EU & schwa |\r\n\r\n**Total: 20 questions. Pass: 65% (13/20).**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 🎉 Ready?\r\n\r\nClick **Finish Mission → Take Exam** to begin.\r\n  	2026-10-14 09:45:03.905328+00	38203b3c-8f0b-4ece-9f3c-3f7686a768c0	t	65
a78220a1-c32a-4e38-9ebc-7a2698afb2ba	\N	Review — V2 Basics (SQ 1–4)	5	\N	\r\n# 🔄 Review — V2 Basics\r\n\r\nFour lessons. One rule. Every main clause.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📚 01 The Rule\r\n\r\n> **Verb sits in position 2. Always.**\r\n\r\nPosition 1 can be anything. Subject stays close behind the verb if something else fronts.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧱 02 Standard vs Fronted\r\n\r\n| Standard | Fronted |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik lees vandaag een boek. | Vandaag lees ik een boek. |\r\n| Hij werkt morgen. | Morgen werkt hij. |\r\n| Wij wonen in Amsterdam. | In Amsterdam wonen wij. |\r\n| Zij drinkt koffie in de ochtend. | In de ochtend drinkt zij koffie. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Practice\r\n\r\nRead each pair aloud. Notice the verb never moves.\r\n\r\n- Ik werk vandaag. ↔ Vandaag werk ik.\r\n- Hij leest elke dag. ↔ Elke dag leest hij.\r\n- Wij eten om 6 uur. ↔ Om 6 uur eten wij.\r\n- Zij gaat morgen naar school. ↔ Morgen gaat zij naar school.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 04 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Front → invert. That is the entire system.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: V2 = verb in position 2. Every main clause. No exceptions.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:11:08.096073+00	ec1cd99a-6317-41f8-a5bb-37f4d5a5c87c	f	80
5566c6e4-cc8c-49cf-8b46-a2fbf0995eba	\N	Multiple Adverbs — Time, Manner, Place	6	\N	\r\n# 🧭 Multiple Adverbs — Time, Manner, Place\r\n\r\nWhen several adverbs stack, Dutch has a preferred order.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Order\r\n\r\n> **Time → Manner → Place**\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik ga morgen met de fiets naar school. | Tomorrow I go to school by bike. |\r\n| Hij werkt vandaag hard in de tuin. | Today he works hard in the garden. |\r\n| Wij eten vanavond rustig thuis. | Tonight we eat quietly at home. |\r\n\r\nTime first. Then how (manner). Then where (place).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 Why This Order\r\n\r\nDutch prioritizes **temporal context** first. Then the "how". Then the "where". English usually reverses (place, manner, time).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 With Fronting\r\n\r\nFront the time → verb → subject → manner → place:\r\n\r\n**Morgen** → **ga** → **ik** → **met de fiets** → **naar school**.\r\n\r\n| Position | Word |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| 1 | Morgen |\r\n| 2 | ga |\r\n| 3 | ik |\r\n| 4 | met de fiets |\r\n| 5 | naar school |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Common Patterns\r\n\r\n- Ik werk **vandaag** **hard** **thuis**.\r\n- Wij reizen **morgen** **met de trein** **naar Utrecht**.\r\n- Zij leest **elke avond** **rustig** **in bed**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Front the time, keep the verb second, then stack the rest. **Time → Manner → Place**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: When stacking adverbs, order is TIME → MANNER → PLACE. "Ik ga morgen met de fiets naar school." Never: "Ik ga naar school met de fiets morgen."\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:11:08.096073+00	ec1cd99a-6317-41f8-a5bb-37f4d5a5c87c	f	80
fc562d5e-6a63-4b38-a8be-527d0c03c447	\N	Negation — Niet vs Geen	7	\N	\r\n# 🚫 Negation — Niet vs Geen\r\n\r\nTwo words for "not". Different jobs.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Split\r\n\r\n> **geen** → negates a noun **without** an article or with "een"\r\n>\r\n> **niet** → negates everything else (verbs, adjectives, phrases)\r\n\r\n| Use | Which |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| een boek | **geen** boek |\r\n| koffie (uncountable) | **geen** koffie |\r\n| honden (plural) | **geen** honden |\r\n| ik werk | ik werk **niet** |\r\n| moe (adj) | **niet** moe |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 Examples\r\n\r\n- Ik heb **geen** boek. (I don't have a book.)\r\n- Ik drink **geen** koffie. (I don't drink coffee.)\r\n- Ik werk **niet** vandaag. (I don't work today.)\r\n- Hij is **niet** moe. (He is not tired.)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Word Order — The Tricky Part\r\n\r\n**niet** usually comes at the END of the sentence (after objects/adverbs):\r\n\r\n| Correct | Wrong |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik ken hem **niet**. | Ik niet ken hem. |\r\n| Ik werk vandaag **niet**. | Ik niet werk vandaag. |\r\n| Wij gaan morgen **niet**. | Wij niet gaan morgen. |\r\n\r\n**geen** goes right before the noun:\r\n\r\n| Correct | Wrong |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik heb **geen** tijd. | Ik heb tijd geen. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 With Fronting\r\n\r\nFront time → verb → subject → **niet** at end.\r\n\r\n**Vandaag** → **werk** → **ik** → **niet**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **geen** before nouns. **niet** at the end of everything else.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Use GEEN before an indefinite noun ("geen boek"). Use NIET at the end of the sentence for everything else ("Ik werk niet").\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:11:08.096073+00	ec1cd99a-6317-41f8-a5bb-37f4d5a5c87c	f	80
6b911c45-1075-4509-a789-022781c0d3e7	\N	Expanding — More Regular Verbs	6	\N	\r\n# 🔧 Expanding — More Regular Verbs\r\n\r\nTen more. Same pattern. No new rules.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The New Ten\r\n\r\n| Dutch | Stem | English |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| vragen | vraag | to ask |\r\n| antwoorden | antwoord | to answer |\r\n| kopen | koop | to buy |\r\n| verkopen | verkoop | to sell |\r\n| betalen | betaal | to pay |\r\n| openen | open | to open |\r\n| sluiten | sluit | to close |\r\n| gebruiken | gebruik | to use |\r\n| wachten | wacht | to wait |\r\n| beginnen | begin | to begin |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 Conjugation Check\r\n\r\n**kopen:** koop · koopt · kopen\r\n\r\n**gebruiken:** gebruik · gebruikt · gebruiken\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Sentences\r\n\r\n- Ik koop brood.\r\n- Hij verkoopt boeken.\r\n- Wij betalen de rekening.\r\n- Jij gebruikt een computer.\r\n- Zij wachten op de trein.\r\n- Ik begin vandaag.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: "Wachten op" = wait for. "Beginnen met" = begin with. Learn the prepositions WITH the verb.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 🎯 Done\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:43:20.883117+00	1168346a-ff41-42bf-bd70-de8aad225554	f	80
4d43c220-b948-41e4-aeef-74cf96033036	\N	Mission 1 Recap — Everything You Built	1	\N	\r\n# 🎯 Mission 1 Recap — Everything You Built\r\n\r\nFour sagas. One foundation. You are not a beginner anymore.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧱 01 What You Own\r\n\r\n| Area | Content |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Cognates | 80+ Dutch words you already recognized |\r\n| Vowels | The full long/short system |\r\n| Diphthongs | IE, IJ, EI, UI, OU, AU, EU |\r\n| Consonants | R, G, SCH, V, W, devoicing |\r\n| Grammar | Articles, pronouns, possessives, object forms |\r\n| Frames | Ik ben / Ik heb / Ik ga |\r\n| Word order | SVO, time-before-object |\r\n| Questions | Verb-first inversion |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📖 02 The Cognate Vault — Saga 1.1\r\n\r\n**Identical:** hand · arm · lip · bed · lamp · water · pen · radio\r\n\r\n**Shifted:** vinger · voet · haar · schouder · tand · neus · mond\r\n\r\n**Home:** tafel · stoel · deur · muur · vloer · raam · boek\r\n\r\n**Nature:** zon · maan · ster · regen · sneeuw · boom · bloem\r\n\r\n**Food:** appel · brood · kaas · melk · koffie · thee · wijn\r\n\r\n**Family:** vader · moeder · broer · zus · zoon · dochter\r\n\r\n**Time:** dag · week · maand · jaar · uur · vandaag · morgen\r\n\r\n**Numbers:** een · twee · drie · vier · vijf · zes · zeven · acht · negen · tien\r\n\r\n**False friends:** brood (bread) · room (cream) · bos (forest) · meer (lake)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎵 03 The Sound Engine — Saga 1.2\r\n\r\n| Vowel | Sound | Anchor |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| A | short "ah" | father clipped |\r\n| AA | long "ah" | father |\r\n| E | short "eh" | bed |\r\n| EE | long "ay" | day |\r\n| O | short "oh" | pot |\r\n| OO | long "oh" | boat |\r\n| OE | "oo" | book |\r\n| U | short "uh" | but |\r\n| UU | rounded "ee" | — |\r\n| IE | "ee" | see |\r\n| IJ / EI | "ay" | day |\r\n| UI | ow→oy | — |\r\n| OU / AU | "ow" | house |\r\n| EU | rounded "uh" | — |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🗣️ 04 Consonants — Saga 1.3\r\n\r\n| Sound | Rule |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| R | Rolling (front) or guttural (throat) |\r\n| G | Throat rasp (Scottish loch) |\r\n| SCH | s + g, not "sh" |\r\n| V | English V (teeth + lip) |\r\n| W | Lips only, no glide |\r\n| TH | t only, no English "th" |\r\n| -en | schwa "uh" |\r\n| Final B/D/V/Z | Devoiced: p / t / f / s |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏗️ 05 Pronouns — Saga 1.4\r\n\r\n| Subject | Object | Possessive |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| ik | mij / me | mijn |\r\n| jij | jou / je | jouw / je |\r\n| u | u | uw |\r\n| hij | hem | zijn |\r\n| zij | haar | haar |\r\n| het | het | — |\r\n| wij | ons | ons / onze |\r\n| jullie | jullie | jullie |\r\n| zij | hen / ze | hun |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 06 The Three Frames\r\n\r\n> **Ik ben** ___.  (state, identity)\r\n>\r\n> **Ik heb** ___.  (possession)\r\n>\r\n> **Ik ga** ___.  (movement, intent)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📐 07 Word Order\r\n\r\n> Subject → Verb → Time → Manner → Place → Object\r\n\r\nExample: **Ik ga morgen met de fiets naar school.**\r\n\r\nQuestion: swap subject and verb. **Ga ik morgen naar school?**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 08 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Mission 1 was not about learning words.\r\n>\r\n> It was about **unlocking the system**.\r\n\r\nYou can now:\r\n- Read any Dutch word aloud and get close\r\n- Guess meaning from cognates\r\n- Recognize sound shifts\r\n- Build simple sentences\r\n- Ask questions\r\n\r\nMission 2 turns this into **fluent sentence construction**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 🎉 Side Quest Complete\r\n\r\nNext: the Mission 1 Exam. 40 questions. 80% to pass.\r\n  	2026-10-14 09:50:31.466226+00	dd7c8691-fc49-4521-a970-a883c4b6430c	f	80
