ee7f21a0-a6d0-4d69-83d2-0514adf05f39	\N	The V2 Rule — Verb in Position 2	1	\N	\r\n# 🎯 The V2 Rule — Verb Always Second\r\n\r\nEvery Dutch sentence has one rule that never breaks: **the verb sits in position 2**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📐 01 Positions, Not People\r\n\r\n> Position 1 = the topic\r\n>\r\n> Position 2 = the verb\r\n>\r\n> Position 3+ = everything else\r\n\r\nThis is the V2 rule. It holds in every main clause.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 Standard Order\r\n\r\n| Position | Word | Function |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| 1 | Ik | subject |\r\n| 2 | lees | verb |\r\n| 3 | een boek | object |\r\n\r\n**Ik lees een boek.** ✅\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Front the Time\r\n\r\nMove time to position 1 → verb still lands in position 2 → subject shifts to position 3.\r\n\r\n| Plain | Fronted |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik lees vandaag een boek. | **Vandaag** lees ik een boek. |\r\n| Hij werkt morgen. | **Morgen** werkt hij. |\r\n| Wij gaan naar school. | **Naar school** gaan wij. |\r\n\r\nThe verb **never moves out of position 2**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 04 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **V2 = Verb always Second.** Everything else can move. The verb cannot.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: In every Dutch main clause, the verb sits in POSITION 2. If something moves to position 1, the verb stays put and the subject swaps behind it.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:11:08.096073+00	ec1cd99a-6317-41f8-a5bb-37f4d5a5c87c	f	80
89939111-1d26-4e3c-8c8f-bb6ac362d587	\N	Inversion — When Subject Swaps	2	\N	\r\n# 🔄 Inversion — When Subject Swaps\r\n\r\nFront the time → verb stays at position 2 → subject slides to position 3.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📐 01 The Swap\r\n\r\n| Standard | Inverted |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik werk **vandaag**. | **Vandaag** werk ik. |\r\n| Hij leest **elke dag**. | **Elke dag** leest hij. |\r\n| Wij eten **om 6 uur**. | **Om 6 uur** eten wij. |\r\n\r\nSubject and time trade places. Verb stays put.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 Why It Feels Weird\r\n\r\nEnglish does this too, but only in formal speech:\r\n\r\n- *Never have I seen such a thing.*\r\n- *Tomorrow will the sun rise.*\r\n\r\nDutch does it **all the time**. Every daily sentence.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 The Pattern\r\n\r\nTime → Verb → Subject → Rest\r\n\r\n**Vandaag** → **werk** → **ik** → **thuis**.\r\n\r\n| Position | Word |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| 1 | Vandaag |\r\n| 2 | werk |\r\n| 3 | ik |\r\n| 4 | thuis |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 More Examples\r\n\r\n- **Morgen** ga ik naar Amsterdam.\r\n- **Elke ochtend** drink ik koffie.\r\n- **Om 3 uur** begint de les.\r\n- **In de zomer** reizen wij naar Spanje.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Inversion is not optional. It is the rule. Front anything → invert.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: When something moves to position 1, the SUBJECT and VERB swap. Verb stays in 2, subject moves to 3.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:11:08.096073+00	ec1cd99a-6317-41f8-a5bb-37f4d5a5c87c	f	80
1ddb89d0-a50b-49a1-9044-675edb729366	\N	Time at the Front — Vandaag, Morgen, Gisteren	3	\N	\r\n# ⏰ Time at the Front — Vandaag, Morgen, Gisteren\r\n\r\nThe most common fronted element is time.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Time Words\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| vandaag | today |\r\n| morgen | tomorrow |\r\n| gisteren | yesterday |\r\n| nu | now |\r\n| straks | later |\r\n| vanavond | tonight |\r\n| vanochtend | this morning |\r\n| volgende week | next week |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 Front Them\r\n\r\nEvery time word can start a sentence:\r\n\r\n| Plain | Fronted |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik ga morgen naar school. | **Morgen** ga ik naar school. |\r\n| Hij werkt vandaag. | **Vandaag** werkt hij. |\r\n| Wij eten vanavond thuis. | **Vanavond** eten wij thuis. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Two Rules Combine\r\n\r\n> Time fronted → verb stays position 2 → subject moves to position 3.\r\n\r\n**Vandaag** → **lees** → **ik** → **een boek**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Two Time Words\r\n\r\nWhen both go to the front, the more specific comes first:\r\n\r\n- **Morgen om 3 uur** ga ik naar de dokter.\r\n- **Vandaag in de middag** eten wij thuis.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Time expressions love position 1. When in doubt, front the time.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Time words (vandaag, morgen, gisteren, vanavond) sit comfortably in position 1. Verb jumps behind them to position 2. Subject lands at position 3.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:11:08.096073+00	ec1cd99a-6317-41f8-a5bb-37f4d5a5c87c	f	80
2e365f2e-eb6e-40ce-b1e9-c31dd3719b88	\N	Fronting More Than Time	4	\N	\r\n# 🎯 Fronting More Than Time\r\n\r\nTime is the most common front, but not the only one.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 Place at Front\r\n\r\n| Plain | Fronted |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik woon in Amsterdam. | **In Amsterdam** woon ik. |\r\n| Wij gaan naar school. | **Naar school** gaan wij. |\r\n| Hij werkt in een kantoor. | **In een kantoor** werkt hij. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 Emphasis at Front\r\n\r\nFront the object to emphasize it:\r\n\r\n| Plain | Fronted |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik lust koffie niet. | **Koffie** lust ik niet. |\r\n| Wij kijken naar films. | **Naar films** kijken wij. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 The Invariant Rule\r\n\r\nWhatever moves to position 1:\r\n\r\n> Verb stays at position 2. Subject moves to position 3.\r\n\r\nFront place → verb → subject → rest.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 When NOT to Front\r\n\r\nDo not front everything. Only front for:\r\n- emphasis\r\n- natural flow (time first is very common)\r\n- variety\r\n\r\nStandard SVO is always safe.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **Front = invert.** Two words, one rule.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Any element (time, place, object, adverb) can front. Whatever front, the verb stays in position 2 and the subject swaps behind it.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:11:08.096073+00	ec1cd99a-6317-41f8-a5bb-37f4d5a5c87c	f	80
ef822958-cee3-42bf-b2c7-633640c56f0b	\N	V2 in Questions	8	\N	\r\n# ❓ V2 in Questions\r\n\r\nQuestions obey V2 too. The verb is still in position 2 — it just swaps with the subject.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 Yes/No Questions\r\n\r\nVerb moves to position **1** in yes/no questions — this is the one V1 exception.\r\n\r\n| Statement | Question |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Jij leest een boek. | **Lees** jij een boek? |\r\n| Hij werkt vandaag. | **Werkt** hij vandaag? |\r\n| Wij gaan naar school. | **Gaan** wij naar school? |\r\n\r\nVerb goes first. Subject goes second. That is V1 (verb first).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 Question-Word Questions = V2\r\n\r\nWith a question word, the verb goes back to position 2.\r\n\r\n| Dutch | Positions |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| **Wat** lees jij? | QW(1) verb(2) subj(3) |\r\n| **Waar** woon jij? | QW(1) verb(2) subj(3) |\r\n| **Wanneer** ga jij? | QW(1) verb(2) subj(3) |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 03 The Full Picture\r\n\r\n| Type | Position 1 | Position 2 | Position 3 |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| Statement | Subject | Verb | Object |\r\n| Fronted | Topic | Verb | Subject |\r\n| Yes/no question | Verb | Subject | Object |\r\n| QW question | QW | Verb | Subject |\r\n\r\nSame V2 soul. Different skin.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Examples\r\n\r\n- **Lees** jij elke dag? (Do you read every day?)\r\n- **Waar** woon jij? (Where do you live?)\r\n- **Wanneer** komt hij? (When is he coming?)\r\n- **Waarom** werk je niet? (Why don't you work?)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> V2 in statements. V1 in yes/no questions. V2 again in QW questions. Three configurations, one verb.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Statements = V2 (subject, verb, ...). Yes/no questions = V1 (verb, subject, ...). Question-word questions = V2 (QW, verb, subject, ...).\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:11:08.096073+00	ec1cd99a-6317-41f8-a5bb-37f4d5a5c87c	f	80
300355dc-b9c1-4079-bddd-56c65a7620a9	\N	Reading Practice — V2 in the Wild	9	\N	\r\n# 📖 Reading Practice — V2 in the Wild\r\n\r\nRead each sentence. Find the verb. Confirm it is in position 2.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 Short Sentences\r\n\r\n**1.** Ik lees elke dag een boek.\r\n\r\n**2.** Vandaag ga ik naar de winkel.\r\n\r\n**3.** Hij werkt morgen thuis.\r\n\r\n**4.** Morgen werkt hij thuis.\r\n\r\n**5.** Wij drinken koffie in de ochtend.\r\n\r\n**6.** In de ochtend drinken wij koffie.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 Medium Sentences\r\n\r\n**7.** Elke vrijdag bezoek ik mijn ouders in Amsterdam.\r\n\r\n**8.** Volgende week reizen wij met de trein naar België.\r\n\r\n**9.** Vanavond eten wij rustig thuis.\r\n\r\n**10.** In de zomer zwemmen wij graag in de zee.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Questions\r\n\r\n**11.** Lees jij elke dag?\r\n\r\n**12.** Waar woon jij?\r\n\r\n**13.** Wanneer ga je naar school?\r\n\r\n**14.** Waarom werk jij vandaag niet?\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Find the Verb\r\n\r\nFor each sentence, name position 1 and position 2:\r\n\r\n- *Vandaag ga ik naar school.* → Position 1: Vandaag, Position 2: ga ✅\r\n- *Ik lees elke dag.* → Position 1: Ik, Position 2: lees ✅\r\n- *Waar woon jij?* → Position 1: Waar, Position 2: woon ✅\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Read every sentence aloud. Count positions on your fingers. Position 2 = verb. Every time.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Before writing any Dutch sentence, ask: where is my verb? If it is not in position 2, rewrite.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:11:08.096073+00	ec1cd99a-6317-41f8-a5bb-37f4d5a5c87c	f	80
