efb5d527-1643-4e5f-9ea3-d750e05e93f6	\N	Yes/No Questions — Verb First	1	\N	\r\n# ❓ Yes/No Questions — Verb First\r\n\r\nThe simplest question form. One swap. No extra words.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Rule\r\n\r\n> Statement = Subject first\r\n>\r\n> Yes/no question = **Verb first**\r\n\r\n| Statement | Question |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Jij leest een boek. | **Lees** jij een boek? |\r\n| Hij werkt vandaag. | **Werkt** hij vandaag? |\r\n| Wij gaan naar school. | **Gaan** wij naar school? |\r\n| Zij drinkt koffie. | **Drinkt** zij koffie? |\r\n\r\nVerb jumps to position 1. Subject slides to position 2.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 No "Do" Support\r\n\r\nEnglish needs *do/does*: "**Do** you read?" Dutch does not.\r\n\r\n> **Lees jij?** = Do you read?\r\n>\r\n> **Werkt hij?** = Does he work?\r\n\r\nThe verb itself does the work.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Intonation\r\n\r\nIn spoken Dutch, intonation rises at the end:\r\n\r\n- **Lees** jij een boek? ↗\r\n- **Werkt** hij vandaag? ↗\r\n\r\nSame sentence shape as English. Same rising melody.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Real Examples\r\n\r\n- **Kom** je morgen?\r\n- **Heb** je tijd?\r\n- **Ga** je naar huis?\r\n- **Wil** je koffie?\r\n- **Woon** je in Amsterdam?\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **Statement → Question = one swap.** Verb and subject trade places. Nothing else changes.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Yes/no question = VERB first, subject second. No "do" helper. "Lees jij een boek?" not "Do jij lees een boek?"\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:32:49.107632+00	53e32ac0-7954-4501-af31-1eff8a69b449	f	80
4ad222ba-9c73-4221-aeee-7b3c5ad1a679	\N	Question Words — Wat, Waar, Wanneer, Wie	2	\N	\r\n# 🔎 Question Words — The Big Four\r\n\r\nFour question words. Half of every conversation.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Big Four\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Wat | What |\r\n| Waar | Where |\r\n| Wanneer | When |\r\n| Wie | Who |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 The Order\r\n\r\n> Question word → Verb → Subject → Rest\r\n\r\n| Dutch | Positions |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| **Wat** lees jij? | QW(1) verb(2) subj(3) |\r\n| **Waar** woon jij? | QW(1) verb(2) subj(3) |\r\n| **Wanneer** ga jij? | QW(1) verb(2) subj(3) |\r\n| **Wie** ben jij? | QW(1) verb(2) subj(3) |\r\n\r\nSame V2 shape. Different opener.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Real Examples\r\n\r\n- **Wat** eet jij? (What do you eat?)\r\n- **Waar** woon jij? (Where do you live?)\r\n- **Wanneer** komt hij? (When is he coming?)\r\n- **Wie** is dat? (Who is that?)\r\n- **Wat** doe jij? (What do you do?)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Wie vs Wat\r\n\r\n> **Wie** → people\r\n>\r\n> **Wat** → things and actions\r\n\r\n- **Wie** is dat? → a person\r\n- **Wat** is dat? → a thing\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Question word first, verb second. Never "Verb + QW". Always "QW + verb".\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Question-word questions use V2 again — QW in position 1, verb in position 2, subject in position 3. "Waar woon jij?" not "Waar jij woont?"\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:32:49.107632+00	53e32ac0-7954-4501-af31-1eff8a69b449	f	80
2a28cf61-e5a0-446c-ac7c-b55adb0eeaa8	\N	Question Words — Waarom, Hoe, Welke	3	\N	\r\n# 🧭 Question Words — The Next Three\r\n\r\nThree more question words. Complete the set.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Three\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Waarom | Why |\r\n| Hoe | How |\r\n| Welke | Which |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 Welke — The Special One\r\n\r\n**Welke** changes form depending on the noun:\r\n\r\n| Noun type | Form | Example |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| de-word | welke | **Welke** man? |\r\n| het-word | welk | **Welk** huis? |\r\n| plural | welke | **Welke** boeken? |\r\n\r\nWhen in doubt, **welke** is safe in most contexts.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Real Examples\r\n\r\n- **Waarom** werk je niet? (Why don't you work?)\r\n- **Hoe** gaat het? (How are you?)\r\n- **Hoe** heet jij? (What is your name? — lit. "How are you called?")\r\n- **Welke** kleur? (Which color?)\r\n- **Waarom** kom je laat? (Why are you coming late?)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Hoe + Adjective\r\n\r\n**Hoe** pairs with adjectives for "how ___":\r\n\r\n- **Hoe** groot? (How big?)\r\n- **Hoe** oud? (How old?)\r\n- **Hoe** ver? (How far?)\r\n- **Hoe** veel? (How much / how many?)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> "Hoe heet jij?" is not "How are you called?" literally — it is the Dutch way to ask someone's name. Learn it as one piece.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: "Hoe heet jij?" = What is your name? Never translate it literally. It is a fixed Dutch phrase.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:32:49.107632+00	53e32ac0-7954-4501-af31-1eff8a69b449	f	80
2f60a935-fcc5-429e-9071-9850f55e38d8	\N	Negation — Niet vs Geen	4	\N	\r\n# 🚫 Negation — Niet vs Geen\r\n\r\nTwo words for "not". Different jobs.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Split\r\n\r\n> **geen** → before nouns without an article (or with "een")\r\n>\r\n> **niet** → everything else (verbs, adjectives, phrases)\r\n\r\n| Use geen | Use niet |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| een boek | ik werk |\r\n| koffie (uncount.) | moe (adj) |\r\n| honden (plural) | hier (place) |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧠 02 Examples\r\n\r\n**geen:**\r\n- Ik heb **geen** boek.\r\n- Ik drink **geen** koffie.\r\n- Wij hebben **geen** tijd.\r\n- Er zijn **geen** stoelen.\r\n\r\n**niet:**\r\n- Ik werk **niet** vandaag.\r\n- Hij is **niet** moe.\r\n- Wij gaan **niet** naar school.\r\n- Dat is **niet** waar.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Word Order\r\n\r\n**geen** sits right before the noun:\r\n\r\n> Ik heb **geen** boek. ✅\r\n>\r\n> Ik heb boek **geen**. ❌\r\n\r\n**niet** usually comes at the END of the sentence (after objects, adverbs):\r\n\r\n> Ik ken hem **niet**. ✅\r\n>\r\n> Ik niet ken hem. ❌\r\n\r\n> Vandaag werk ik **niet**. ✅\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 With Fronting\r\n\r\nFront time → verb → subject → **niet** at end:\r\n\r\n**Vandaag** → **werk** → **ik** → **niet**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **geen** = "no" attached to a thing.\r\n>\r\n> **niet** = "not" attached to everything else.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: GEEN before indefinite nouns ("geen boek", "geen koffie"). NIET at the end of the sentence for everything else ("Ik werk niet", "Ik ken hem niet").\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:32:49.107632+00	53e32ac0-7954-4501-af31-1eff8a69b449	f	80
f81f94cc-2096-4f67-aad9-f6c4fda6659c	\N	Review — Questions & Negation (SQ 1–4)	5	\N	\r\n# 🔄 Review — Questions & Negation\r\n\r\nFour lessons. All question types. Both negations.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📚 01 The Question Types\r\n\r\n| Type | Position 1 | Position 2 | Position 3 |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| Statement | Subject | Verb | Object |\r\n| Yes/no Q | Verb | Subject | Object |\r\n| QW question | QW | Verb | Subject |\r\n\r\nSame V2 skeleton. Three configurations.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 The Question Words\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Wat | What |\r\n| Waar | Where |\r\n| Wanneer | When |\r\n| Wie | Who |\r\n| Waarom | Why |\r\n| Hoe | How |\r\n| Welke / Welk | Which |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Negation\r\n\r\n| Type | Word | Position |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Indefinite noun | geen | Before noun |\r\n| Everything else | niet | End of sentence |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Mixed Practice\r\n\r\nRead aloud:\r\n\r\n- **Lees jij een boek?** — Do you read a book?\r\n- **Waar woon jij?** — Where do you live?\r\n- **Waarom werk je niet?** — Why don't you work?\r\n- **Ik heb geen tijd.** — I have no time.\r\n- **Ik ken hem niet.** — I don't know him.\r\n- **Hoe heet jij?** — What is your name?\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **Question = verb move. Negation = word choice. Two systems. Master both.**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Three configurations — Statement (subj-verb), yes/no (verb-subj), QW (QW-verb-subj). Negation — geen before indefinite nouns, niet at the end.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:32:49.107632+00	53e32ac0-7954-4501-af31-1eff8a69b449	f	80
66f42880-c698-45d1-9284-560627675918	\N	Short Answers — Ja, Nee, Toch	6	\N	\r\n# ✅ Short Answers — Ja, Nee, Toch\r\n\r\nDutch replies to yes/no questions follow a simple pattern.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Basic Reply\r\n\r\n> Echo the verb. Positive → **ja**. Negative → **nee**.\r\n\r\n| Question | Answer |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Lees jij? | **Ja**, ik lees. |\r\n| Lees jij? | **Nee**, ik lees niet. |\r\n| Werk je? | **Ja**, ik werk. |\r\n| Werk je? | **Nee**, ik werk niet. |\r\n\r\nDo not just say "Ja." Echo the verb for clarity.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 "Toch" — The Confirmation\r\n\r\n**Toch** = a soft "right?" / "isn't it?" at the end.\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Je komt, **toch**? | You are coming, right? |\r\n| Dat is waar, **toch**? | That is true, isn't it? |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 "Wel" and "Niet" as Emphasis\r\n\r\n**Wel** = emphasize YES against doubt.\r\n**Niet** = emphasize NO against doubt.\r\n\r\n| Sentence | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik **wil** wel. | I DO want to. |\r\n| Ik wil **niet**. | I do NOT want to. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Common Reply Words\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ja | Yes |\r\n| Nee | No |\r\n| Natuurlijk | Of course |\r\n| Zeker | Certainly |\r\n| Misschien | Maybe |\r\n| Oké | OK |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **Echo the verb.** Not "Ja." but "Ja, ik lees." Two words beat one.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Short answer = Ja/Nee + verb echo. "Ja, ik lees." not just "Ja." Keeps the conversation clear.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:32:49.107632+00	53e32ac0-7954-4501-af31-1eff8a69b449	f	80
7c85dabc-5c9c-4a79-8342-91bccb959be5	\N	Negation Deep Dive — Placement Rules	7	\N	\r\n# 🎯 Negation Deep Dive — Where Exactly?\r\n\r\nYou learned geen / niet. Now the placement precision.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Default\r\n\r\n> **niet** goes at the END of the sentence.\r\n\r\n| Correct | Wrong |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik ken hem **niet**. | Ik niet ken hem. |\r\n| Ik werk vandaag **niet**. | Ik niet werk vandaag. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 When "niet" Comes Earlier\r\n\r\n**niet** shifts left when negating a SPECIFIC element:\r\n\r\n| Sentence | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik woon **niet** in Amsterdam, maar in Utrecht. | Not in Amsterdam (specifically) |\r\n| Ik ga **niet** morgen, maar overmorgen. | Not tomorrow (specifically) |\r\n\r\nPattern: contrast → niet moves left.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 "geen" Never Moves\r\n\r\n**geen** always sits immediately before the noun:\r\n\r\n| Correct | Wrong |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik heb **geen** tijd. | Ik heb tijd geen. |\r\n| Wij hebben **geen** geld. | Wij hebben geld geen. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Double Negation Trap\r\n\r\nEnglish allows double negatives. Dutch does too, but carefully:\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Ik heb **nooit geen** tijd. | Wrong — sounds like uneducated |\r\n| Ik heb **nooit** tijd. | Correct |\r\n| Ik heb **geen** tijd. | Correct |\r\n\r\n> One negation per sentence is the safe rule.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 05 With Questions\r\n\r\nNegate a question:\r\n\r\n- **Kom** je **niet**? — Are you not coming?\r\n- **Heb** je **geen** tijd? — Do you have no time?\r\n\r\nVerb first, negation after subject or before noun.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 06 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **niet at the end.** Move it left only to contrast.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: NIET sits at the END of the sentence. Move it left only when you are contrasting with a specific word. GEEN never moves — always directly before the noun.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:32:49.107632+00	53e32ac0-7954-4501-af31-1eff8a69b449	f	80
89622211-5fe7-44e3-b77a-65bdf6233eca	\N	Question Tags & Confirmations	8	\N	\r\n# 🗣️ Question Tags & Confirmations\r\n\r\nNatural Dutch conversation uses a few small tags. Learn them and sound human.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 The Tags\r\n\r\n| Tag | Use | Example |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| toch | Soft "right?" | Je komt, **toch**? |\r\n| hè | Casual "huh?" / "right?" | Lekker, **hè**? |\r\n| ja | Confirm | Dat is waar, **ja**. |\r\n| nee | Deny | Dat is niet waar, **nee**. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 Toch vs Hè\r\n\r\n> **toch** = formal check (do you agree?)\r\n>\r\n> **hè** = casual, almost like a sigh of agreement\r\n\r\n- Dat is waar, **toch**? — That is true, right?\r\n- Lekker weer, **hè**? — Nice weather, huh?\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Negative Tags\r\n\r\nDutch often adds **niet** at the end for negative tags:\r\n\r\n| Dutch | English |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Je komt, **toch**? | You are coming, right? |\r\n| Je komt **niet**, **toch**? | You are not coming, right? |\r\n\r\nThe position of **niet** changes the meaning.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Real Conversation\r\n\r\n- **Jij woont in Amsterdam, toch?** — You live in Amsterdam, right?\r\n- **Ja, klopt.** — Yes, correct.\r\n- **Je spreekt geen Nederlands, toch?** — You don't speak Dutch, right?\r\n- **Nee, ik spreek geen Nederlands.** — No, I don't speak Dutch.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> **Toch** = polite tag. **Hè** = friendly tag. Both add warmth to a sentence.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Add "toch?" at the end of a statement to turn it into a soft confirmation. "Jij woont in Amsterdam, toch?" = "You live in Amsterdam, right?"\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:32:49.107632+00	53e32ac0-7954-4501-af31-1eff8a69b449	f	80
df782802-a012-4a4e-9be5-8e40a1eeea8a	\N	Reading Practice — Real Dialogues	9	\N	\r\n# 📖 Reading Practice — Real Dialogues\r\n\r\nTwo people. Real questions. Real answers. Real negation.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 01 Simple Dialogue — Café\r\n\r\n**A:** Goedemorgen! **Wat wilt u?**\r\n*Good morning! What would you like?*\r\n\r\n**B:** **Ik wil graag koffie**, alstublieft.\r\n*I would like coffee, please.*\r\n\r\n**A:** **Met melk?**\r\n*With milk?*\r\n\r\n**B:** **Nee, geen melk. Zwart, graag.**\r\n*No, no milk. Black, please.*\r\n\r\n**A:** **Komt er nog iets bij?**\r\n*Anything else?*\r\n\r\n**B:** **Nee, dat is alles. Dank u.**\r\n*No, that is all. Thank you.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 02 Dialogue — Meeting Someone\r\n\r\n**A:** **Hoe heet jij?**\r\n*What is your name?*\r\n\r\n**B:** **Ik heet Lisa. En jij?**\r\n*My name is Lisa. And you?*\r\n\r\n**A:** **Yassine. Waar woon jij?**\r\n*Yassine. Where do you live?*\r\n\r\n**B:** **Ik woon in Rotterdam. En jij?**\r\n*I live in Rotterdam. And you?*\r\n\r\n**A:** **Amsterdam. Werk je of studeer je?**\r\n*Amsterdam. Do you work or study?*\r\n\r\n**B:** **Ik werk. Ik heb geen tijd om te studeren.**\r\n*I work. I don't have time to study.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 03 Dialogue — Making Plans\r\n\r\n**A:** **Ga je mee naar de film?**\r\n*Are you coming to the movie?*\r\n\r\n**B:** **Wanneer?**\r\n*When?*\r\n\r\n**A:** **Vanavond om 8 uur.**\r\n*Tonight at 8.*\r\n\r\n**B:** **Nee, vanavond kan ik niet. Morgen?**\r\n*No, tonight I can't. Tomorrow?*\r\n\r\n**A:** **Morgen is goed. Kom je alleen?**\r\n*Tomorrow is good. Are you coming alone?*\r\n\r\n**B:** **Nee, ik neem een vriend mee.**\r\n*No, I'm bringing a friend.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 04 Practice Patterns\r\n\r\nNotice the recurring structure:\r\n- QW → verb → subject (Waar woon jij?)\r\n- Verb → subject (Kom je mee?)\r\n- geen / niet for negation\r\n- toch? / hè? for tags\r\n\r\nRead each dialogue aloud. Feel the rhythm.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 05 Meridian Insight\r\n\r\n> Real Dutch is questions + answers + small negations. Nothing fancy. Master the pattern, master the conversation.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> ✍️ WRITE THIS DOWN: Real Dutch = "Waar woon jij?" / "Ik woon in Amsterdam." / "En jij?" / "Nee, ik werk." These four shapes carry 90% of first conversations.\r\n  	2026-10-14 11:32:49.107632+00	53e32ac0-7954-4501-af31-1eff8a69b449	f	80
